{"kind":"report","text":"CORRECTED CHESS LANDING-BOUNDARY REPORT. The recommendation is unchanged - gitseq-chess gets its own workroom - and the migration in my first report was wrong about who owns what. Supersedes 6ab9b6f2.\n\nTHE CORRECTION, VERIFIED RATHER THAN ACCEPTED. My first report said the artifacts to retire were \"codex for all four at 73a5ffb6 and for the b86eeb8b pair\". I resolved every id against the projection before agreeing: 9178d03e at cmd/chess and e48437cf at README.md, both at commit b86eeb8b, are authored by PLANNER. They are mine, from my own chess key-custody lane, and I attributed them to codex. Codex owns exactly the four at 73a5ffb6: 29e78961 cmd/chess, b19f450b chess.go, a0028c3f chess.go, e2264f30 README.md. Getting retirement ownership wrong is not cosmetic - a bare supersede is admitted only from the author, so my migration as written would have refused on two of six.\n\nTHE RECOMMENDATION STANDS, and the evidence for it is unchanged. host/ already models one repository bound to one log with one application, host.Init writes genesis and binding into that repository own common dir, and host/external_test.go builds a real second module to prove the exported surface suffices. gitseq-chess has no .git/gitseq of its own, so chess actors write into this workroom - that is the anomaly, not a gap in the tool. validateCheckout at cmd/gs/main.go:945-954 compares Git COMMON directories and gates gs review and gs merge alike, which is why my own item-4 verdict had to be hand-filed. And the only thing currently stopping a core merge from retiring the chess README artifacts is successionPredecessors reading exit 128 as not-an-ancestor.\n\nTHE SEQUENCE, in order, with who acts at each step.\n\nOne: HUGH bootstraps. host.Init in gitseq-chess, creating that repository own genesis and binding. This is hugh because it mints a log and its first membership, and no agent should do that for a new application.\n\nTwo: LOCAL request and promise in the new log. Chess work is re-requested and claimed there, natively. Nothing in the new log rests on an id from this one.\n\nThree: FOREIGN IDS ARE PROSE ONLY. An event id from this workroom cannot be a causal basis in the chess log - different genesis, so it does not resolve. Where the chess log needs to refer to work done here, it quotes the id in text and says which log it belongs to. That is a real cost of the separation and it is the honest way to pay it.\n\nFour: AUTHOR-CORRECT ROOT CLOSURES here. I retire my two, 9178d03e and e48437cf, with a bare supersede naming no successor, because their paths are not paths this repository keeps. Codex retires its four at 73a5ffb6 the same way. I own the three root requests and close them here.\n\nFive: NEVER, and these are absolute. Core README artifact 09801426 is not retired as chess succession - it is the gitseq README and the whole reason this matters. Design artifact fe8507be at notes/2026-08-13-second-application.md is not retired either. And no manual bypass of gs merge at any step.\n\nWHAT I STILL WANT ON THE RECORD AGAINST MY OWN RECOMMENDATION. Chess durable history stays in this log: the item-4 lineage, the verdicts, my review from earlier tonight. A separate workroom does not move it, and anyone tracing that work later crosses logs by hand, now with foreign ids as prose on both sides. If that is judged unacceptable, the answer is scoped artifact identity and its migration, and that should be decided deliberately rather than by default.\n\nAND ONE DEFECT INDEPENDENT OF THE CHOICE. successionPredecessors at cmd/gs/succession.go:205 treats any git merge-base error, including exit 128 for an unresolvable commit, as not-an-ancestor. That is a silent wrong answer in merge planning. It should refuse loudly whatever we decide here, and I will file it separately.","body":{"commit":"d91fb8b633d78b224c388eb96098825d9fd42c4b"}}